2. The facts leading to the issuance of the Rule in a nutshell can be stated thus, the predecessor of the present opposite party Nos.1-11 as plaintiffs initially instituted Title Suit being No. 420 of 1978 in the 1st Court of the then Subordinate Judge, Dhaka for getting partition of the suit land in respect of a separate Saham of 6.3 &frac12; acres of land. That suit was contested by the present petitioners who are the defendant Nos. 7-16 of the suit. In that suit the defendant Nos.19-20 also contested by filing separate written statement. That suit subsequently was transferred to the 1st Court of subordinate Judge, Gazipur and was renumbered as Title Suit No. 967 of 1985 and after trial the suit was decreed on 28.02.1990 in part allocating a Saham of 4.02&frac12;&nbsp; acres of land in favour of the plaintiffs. Being aggrieved the plaintiff-opposite parties preferred Title Appeal No. 72 of 1990 in the court of learned District Judge, Gazipur while the defendant-petitioners did not prefer any cross- objection against the judgment and decree. The appeal was transferred to the 1st Court of learned Additional District Judge, Gazipur and after hearing, having found that the issues not being properly adjudicated allowed the appeal and sent back the suit on remand to the trial court. The learned Subordinate Judge 1st Court, Gazipur after getting the suit on remand renumbered the suit as Title Suit No. 112 of 1994 and after hearing passed the judgment and decree dated 16.04.1997 allocating the Saham of 6.03&frac12;&nbsp; acres of land in favour of the plaintiff-opposite parties with further allocation of a Saham of .41 acres of land in favor of the defendant No.19-20. The case of opposite-parties as plaintiffs of Civil Suit No. 112 of 1994 is such that the disputed property originally belonged to one Osman, Abdul, Abbas and Sadhu in equal shares who are four full brothers. The C.S. Khatian stands recorded in their names in four anas share each but the name of four brother&#39;s father Sk. Mohor has been wrongly written in the remark column. The further case of the plaintiff-opposite-parties is such that Sheik Mohar Ali was not alive at the time of C.S. operation. The C.S. Khatian Nos.06 and 46 though was recorded in the name of Sheikh Mahar Ali but in fact that would have been recorded in the name of two sons of sheikh Mohar, Alefuddin and Ainuddin. Subsequently after the death of Osman his two sons and the defendant No.6 and one daughter Moyoarjan along with mother Motijan Bibi inherited the property. One son Abdul Hamid died leaving behind six sons the defendants No. 1-5 and one Mazharul Haque. Mazharul Haque transferred his share in favour of the plaintiff No.1 and the plaintiff No.1 exhausted that land after transferring the same in favour of the defendants. Abbas died leaving two brothers but during his life time he transferred his entire share in favour of his brother Sadhu by a transfer deed dated 13.10.1943. Subsequently Abdul left this country for Assam of India and used to reside therein with his family. The share of Abdul in C.S. Khatian No. 43 has been auction sold in favour one Amullaa Ghosh. Abdur Rahim purchased the land form Amulla and the successor of Abdul Hamid, Sirajul transferred his share in favour of his five brothers and Nurul Haque transferred his share in favour his four brothers Sadhu died leaving one son the plaintiff No. 1 and two daughters Moziran and Kulshum. The two daughters of Sadhu transferred their share in favour of the plaintiff No.1. During the life time of Sadhu his one daughter Sabjan died and as such the heirs of sabjan does not inherit the property of sheikh Sadhu. The wife of Sheikh Sadhu made a Heba-Bil-Ewaj deed in respect of 2 arias share in favour of Mofizuddin on 20.11.1960. Kulshum Bibi transferred her share in favour of the plaintiff Nos. 2-5 and the daughter of Abdul Humid Kadiron Nesa transferred her share in favour of the defendants. The property left behind Sheikh Sadhu was inherited by the plaintiffs who acquiring the suit land by way of inheritance and purchase possess the same in ejmaili with the defendants. The plaintiffs by way of several purchase deeds acquired the land in the disputed holding and the land measuring 5.85 acres had been owning and possessing by the plaintiffs. Furthermore; in respect of the land described in schedule &quot;Kha&quot; the original owners were Sheikh Osman and Sheikh Sadhu in 8 anas share each. The plaintiffs possessing their land with the heirs of Sheikh Osman. In respect of 24 decimals of land in plot No.99 and 0.27 decimals of land in plot No. 86 of the C.S. Khatian Na. 43 one surendra Nath purchased before C.S. operation and that land has been excluded from the schedule of the plaint. During S.A. and R.S. operation the land has been recorded in ejmali with the names of the defendants. The plaintiffs due to their difficulty in ejmali possession requested the defendants to make amicable partition but being failure instituted the suit.&nbsp; 

 7. The learned Advocate appearing on behalf of the petitioners submits that the learned Courts below committed error of law in deciding the fate of the Suit and its subsequent appeal in favour of the plaintiffs which is not legal, just and proper. The learned Advocate further submits that the learned Appellate court failed to assess the evidence on records and appreciate the actual position of lass in its true perspective and that there has been non-consideration of material facts as well as misreading and non-reading of evidence resulting in an error in the decision occasioning failure of justice. The learned Advocate also submits that the trial court committed error of law in holding that the suit land belonged to Osman Ali and his three brothers which is against with the pleadings of the parties and evidence on records and both the courts below wrongly held that the defendants admitted the ownership of Osman of his brothers in the suit land rather the defendant Nos.7-16, 19-20 and 25 clearly asserted in their written statements that the suit land originally belonged to Sheikh Mohar Ali, father of Osman and his brothers. The same is also appears in the plaint of a plaintiffs and it has been stated by them that the name of Sheikh Mohar Ali has been wrongly recorded in the remark column of the khatian. The learned Advocate further submits that all the P.Ws. during their deposition admitted the possession of the defendants in the disputed property which is not considered by the courts below and the concerned C.S. Khatian marked exhibit -1 shows that the suit land belonged to Sheikh Mohar Ali. In respect of getting separate Saham in the suit holding the question of possession in allocating Saham in favour of the defendant-petitioners has been ignored by the courts below and in this context the learned Advocate referred a decision of our Apex Court reported in 17 DLR (AD) 57 wherein it is held that under Section 115 of the Code of Civil Procedure the High Court Division is empowered to interfere with the concurrent observation &amp; findings of the courts below when it is noticed that there has been error of law or misreading &amp; non-reading of material evidence resulting in an error in the decision occasioning failure of justice. Moreover; the evidence which were adduced from the sides of the respective parties not properly evaluated by the learned court in allocation of separate Sahain and without cancellation of kabala dated 3.11.1943 marked Exhibit- &quot;চ&quot; the plaintiffs are not in position to have any relief as prayed for. It is further submitted from the side of the learned counsel for the petitioners that the claim of the plaintiffs is absolutely false, fabricated and motivated in view of their own admission made in their title deed marked Exhibit-4, where in they have asserted that they obtained the suit land by way of inheritance. P.W.1 admits that the name of Sheikh Mohor has been inserted in the C.S. khotian and thereby it is clear that the claim of the plaintiffs is barred by the principle of estoppel and it can be legitimately held that the suit land belonged to Sheikh Mohar Ali, who died leaving four sons and three daughters which is admitted by P.W. 1 in his cross examination. These sons and daughters of Sheikh Mohar Ali inherited the suit land in the following manner in schedule&nbsp; 

 11. As against the aforesaid submissions of the learned Advocate for the petitioners the learned Advocate appearing on behalf of the opposite patties submits that in the disputed holding the area of total land is 9.11 acres and out of that the plaintiff-opposite-parties prayed for a separate Saham comprising an area of 6.03 &frac14; acres of land. He further submits on behalf of the opposite-parties that both the trial court as well as the appellate court in their findings and decisions evaluated all the evidence on records correctly in its total approach and perspective. In sifting the evidence both oral and documentary and the assessment of evidence which is done is proper and lawful in the eye of law. There is no misreading or non-reading of evidence and non-consideration of material facts resulting in an error in the decision occasioning failure of justice by which it can be held that the plaintiffs are not entitled to get the relief in allocating their separate Salim as prayed for. It is the concurrent findings of the courts below that the deed dated 03.11.1943 marked exhibit- &quot;Cha&quot; from the side of the defendants is not a genuine document. It is antedated and beyond the pleadings &#39;of the defendants. The C.S. khatian 43 which is Exhibit -1 is not challenged form the side of the defendants and it stands recorded in the names of four brothers in 4 (four) anas share each. This khatian has a presumptive value of correctness and if it is not correct the other heirs of Sheikh Mohar would have been raised objection against it. In this context; the learned Advocate referred a decision of a Division Bench reported in 61 DLR 18. It appears from the connected documents that Exhibit-4 was executed on 27.09.1943 and its registration has been completed under Section 60 of the Registration Act on 13.12.1943 A.D. thence, Exhibit 4 was executed earlier with that of Exhibit &quot;Cha&quot; and both the two documents appears to have been executed by the same vendor. Under Section 47 of the Registration Act Exhibit-4 is a valid document which was duly effected on the date of its execution. Furthermore; it is concurrent findings of both the courts below that Mozammel and Mozaharul Haque is the some individual having two names.&nbsp; 

 18. Scrutinizing the connected papers on records it further appears that both the courts below considering the C.S. khatians as well as the fact of gifting &frac14; land of the Khatian by Abbas vide Ext-&quot;Cha&quot; and of &frac14;&nbsp; land of the Khatian in the name of Abdul being sold in auction, concurrently held that Osman, Abbas, Abdul and Sadhu were the owners of the suit land and the daughters of Sheikh Mohar had no subsisting interest in the said land. Therefore, no title would be conferred upon the defendants, those who purchased of land from the daughters of Sheikh Mohar. In this context the provisions of sections 143, 144 and 144A of the state Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950 are relevant and the decision reported in 61 DLR 18 (Para-30) as referred from the side of the opposite-parties can be relied on.&nbsp;
